{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 2, :comments ["<b>When you mount</b> — Why is the pericope of the Menorah juxtaposed to the inauguration of the princes? Because when Aaron saw the inauguration of the princes, his spirit grew weak that he was not with them in the inauguration — neither he nor his tribe. HaShem said to him: By your life, yours is greater than theirs, for you kindle and prepare the lamps morning and evening — this is the language of Rashi (Rashi on Numbers 8:2), from a midrash of the Aggadah. Now it is not clear to me why they comforted him with the kindling of the lamps and did not comfort him with the incense offering morning and evening, in which Scripture praises him: \"They shall place incense in Your nostril\" (Deuteronomy 33:10) — and with all the offerings, and with the meal-offering of the flat cakes, and with the service of Yom HaKippurim which is not valid except through him, and that he enters the Holy of Holies, and that he is the holy one of YHWH standing in His sanctuary to minister before Him and to bless in His name, and that his entire tribe are all ministers of our Elohim? And furthermore: what reason could there be for this faintness of spirit? Was not his offering greater than those of the princes, for during those days he offered many offerings throughout all the days of inauguration! And if you should say that those were obligatory and he was commanded concerning them, and his spirit grew weak because he did not bring a freewill offering like theirs for the inauguration of the altar — the kindling of the lamps with which they comforted him is likewise obligatory and he was commanded concerning it! Rather, the intent of this Aggadah is to derive a hint from the pericope concerning the inauguration of the lamps that took place in the Second Temple through Aaron and his sons — I mean the Hasmonean High Priest and his sons. And in this language I found it in Megillat Setarim by Rabbenu Nissim, who cited this Aggadah and said: I saw in the midrash — when the twelve tribes brought offerings and the tribe of Levi did not bring, etc. — HaShem said to Moses: Speak to Aaron and say to him, there is another inauguration in which there is kindling of lamps, and I will perform for Israel through your sons miracles and deliverance and an inauguration called by their name, and it is the inauguration of the sons of the Hasmoneans; therefore this pericope is juxtaposed to the pericope of the inauguration of the altar — end of his quote. I also saw in Yelammedenu (Tanchuma, BeHa'alotkha 5), and likewise in Bamidbar Rabbah (15:6): The Holy One, blessed be He, said to Moses: Go, say to Aaron, Do not fear — for greatness greater than this you are destined; the offerings, as long as the Temple stands, are practiced, but the lamps — forever they shall give light toward the face of the Menorah — and all the blessings that I have given you to bless My children are never abolished forever. Now it is a well-known matter that when the Temple is not standing and the offerings are abolished because of its destruction, the lamps too are abolished; rather, they alluded only to the lamps of the inauguration of the Hasmoneans, which is practiced even after the destruction in our exile. So too the Priestly Blessing, which is juxtaposed to the inauguration of the princes, is practiced forever; they derived that sections are juxtaposed to the inauguration of the princes, before it and after it, for the honor of Aaron, who was not counted with them.\n\nAnd R' Avraham said (Ibn Ezra on Numbers 8:2) that this pericope is juxtaposed to inform that the divine speech would occur also at night, for there the lamp would be burning and would not be extinguished. This is not so according to the opinion of our Sages, who said (Mekhilta, Bo, chapter 1) — was He not spoken with only by day? And if R' Avraham had known the difference between the prophecy of Moses and the prophecy of the rest of the prophets, he would not have thought thus. That is what Scripture says: \"In a vision to Him I make Myself known; in a dream I speak with him. Not so My servant Moses\" (Numbers 12:6–8) — that his prophecy is not in a dream, for a dream is literally at night.\n\nRather, the arrangement of these pericopes is as I explained (at the beginning of the book): Scripture comes in this book to complete the law of the offerings and everything obligated to be done in the Tent of Meeting. Now first it said: \"And you shall command, and they shall take to you pure olive oil to kindle a lamp continually\" (Exodus 27:20), and there it did not mention the Menorah, and the implication was that they would kindle on the Menorah when it was available, as it says in its fashioning: \"And he shall mount its lamps and give light on its face\" (Exodus 25:37); but if perhaps it should be lost or broken, they would kindle without a Menorah, and the Menorah does not prevent the kindling, for the commandment is to kindle a lamp continually forever. Afterward He further commanded immediately and for all generations: \"Command the children of Israel and they shall take to you pure olive oil\" (Leviticus 24:2), and said: \"On the pure Menorah he shall arrange the lamps\" — that he shall arrange only on the pure Menorah. And here, when He completed mentioning the erection of the Tabernacle, He further completed all the laws of the lamps, and commanded that the seven lamps should all be burning, for all generations, toward the face of the Menorah, as He mentioned in the fashioning of the Menorah: \"And he shall mount its lamps and give light on its face\" — not without a Menorah, and not unless they all give light toward its face. And He did not mention \"in the Tent of Meeting\" in this pericope, to teach that it would apply also in the Temple; one might think that because there are no windows in the Tent of Meeting it would need this light, but in the Temple, where there would be \"recessed windows\" (I Kings 6:4), it would not be necessary — therefore He did not mention here \"in the Tent of Meeting.\""], :book :numbers, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 8, :notes {0 "Where Ramban says 'ואולי יחשוב' (one might think), the logical context indicates this is the erroneous reasoning that Scripture comes to forestall by omitting 'in the Tent of Meeting.'"}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T04:04:02.444630094Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}